1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,694
You walk into a supermarket for one thing. Milk.

2
00:00:04,194 --> 00:00:08,892
Twenty minutes later, you're at the checkout with snacks, a scented candle,

3
00:00:08,892 --> 00:00:12,024
two bags of chips, and somehow, no milk.

4
00:00:12,624 --> 00:00:16,099
That's not bad luck. And it's not a lack of willpower.

5
00:00:16,479 --> 00:00:20,069
From the moment you walk in, almost everything around you has been

6
00:00:20,069 --> 00:00:23,659
designed to get you to spend more. The music. The smells. Even

7
00:00:23,659 --> 00:00:25,454
the numbers on the price tags.

8
00:00:25,904 --> 00:00:28,338
And most of it works on a part of your brain you

9
00:00:28,338 --> 00:00:28,946
don't even notice.

10
00:00:29,746 --> 00:00:34,896
The average American supermarket carries more than thirty thousand different products. Thirty

11
00:00:34,896 --> 00:00:35,325
thousand.

12
00:00:35,825 --> 00:00:40,184
No brain can carefully think through that many choices. So it doesn't.

13
00:00:40,184 --> 00:00:41,637
It switches to autopilot.

14
00:00:42,017 --> 00:00:46,187
And supermarkets have spent decades learning exactly how to steer that autopilot.

15
00:00:46,787 --> 00:00:50,140
So, how do supermarkets trick your brain into spending more?

16
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:55,247
Let's start with the thing you're pushing. In 1937, an Oklahoma grocery

17
00:00:55,247 --> 00:00:59,254
store owner named Sylvan Goldman introduced one of the first shopping carts.

18
00:00:59,634 --> 00:01:03,416
And at first, nobody wanted to use it. Women said it felt

19
00:01:03,416 --> 00:01:06,567
like pushing a baby carriage. Men thought it looked unmanly.

20
00:01:07,017 --> 00:01:11,197
So Goldman hired people to pretend to shop with the carts, until

21
00:01:11,197 --> 00:01:12,938
real customers started copying them.

22
00:01:13,388 --> 00:01:16,881
It worked. And it showed something stores have used ever since. When

23
00:01:16,881 --> 00:01:20,375
your hands are free and your basket is bigger, you tend to

24
00:01:20,375 --> 00:01:20,957
buy more.

25
00:01:21,557 --> 00:01:25,403
Now look at where things are. Milk, eggs, and bread, the stuff

26
00:01:25,403 --> 00:01:28,929
almost everyone needs, are usually at the back of the store.

27
00:01:29,309 --> 00:01:32,572
Part of that is practical, since the fridges and deliveries are in

28
00:01:32,572 --> 00:01:35,835
the back. But it also means that to grab one carton of

29
00:01:35,835 --> 00:01:38,010
milk, you walk past thousands of other things.

30
00:01:38,510 --> 00:01:42,451
Then there's the entrance. Shoppers barely register the first few steps inside

31
00:01:42,451 --> 00:01:45,078
the door. Retailers call it the decompression zone.

32
00:01:45,458 --> 00:01:49,410
So that's often where stores put something beautiful. Bright fruit, fresh flowers,

33
00:01:49,410 --> 00:01:53,362
the smell of baking bread. It's meant to slow you down and

34
00:01:53,362 --> 00:01:57,314
put you in a good mood, before you've even grabbed a basket.

35
00:01:58,214 --> 00:02:01,641
Once you've slowed down, the store goes to work on your senses.

36
00:02:02,091 --> 00:02:06,368
In a classic 1982 study, researcher Ronald Milliman played slow or fast

37
00:02:06,368 --> 00:02:07,794
music in a supermarket.

38
00:02:08,174 --> 00:02:12,192
With slow music, shoppers walked more slowly, and the store sold about

39
00:02:12,192 --> 00:02:14,871
thirty-eight percent more than on fast music days.

40
00:02:15,371 --> 00:02:19,924
Even the style of music matters. In 1997, British psychologists played French

41
00:02:19,924 --> 00:02:22,959
or German music next to a wine display.

42
00:02:23,339 --> 00:02:27,710
On French music days, French wine outsold German wine by about five

43
00:02:27,710 --> 00:02:30,988
to one. On German music days, German wine won.

44
00:02:31,438 --> 00:02:34,496
And when shoppers were asked, they seemed to have no idea the

45
00:02:34,496 --> 00:02:36,534
music had anything to do with their choice.

46
00:02:37,234 --> 00:02:40,506
Then come the shelves. The products at eye level are often the

47
00:02:40,506 --> 00:02:43,778
ones the store most wants you to buy, and brands can pay

48
00:02:43,778 --> 00:02:44,596
for that space.

49
00:02:44,976 --> 00:02:48,414
Cheaper store brands are often hiding up high, or down near the

50
00:02:48,414 --> 00:02:51,852
floor. Meanwhile, the sugary cereals sit right where a kid's eyes are.

51
00:02:52,352 --> 00:02:55,747
And the ends of the aisles, called end caps, are prime real

52
00:02:55,747 --> 00:02:59,142
estate. Products there tend to sell much more, even when the price

53
00:02:59,142 --> 00:03:02,254
isn't any lower, because a big display feels like a deal.

54
00:03:03,254 --> 00:03:07,205
But some of the sneakiest tricks aren't on the shelves. They're in

55
00:03:07,205 --> 00:03:07,864
the numbers.

56
00:03:08,364 --> 00:03:12,190
Why does everything cost two ninety-nine instead of three dollars? Because your

57
00:03:12,190 --> 00:03:16,015
brain reads prices from left to right, and it latches onto that

58
00:03:16,015 --> 00:03:16,653
first digit.

59
00:03:17,033 --> 00:03:21,238
Two ninety-nine just feels closer to two than to three.

60
00:03:21,738 --> 00:03:25,972
In one famous experiment, researchers changed the price of a dress in

61
00:03:25,972 --> 00:03:30,205
a clothing catalog. At thirty-four dollars, it sold. At thirty-nine dollars, it

62
00:03:30,205 --> 00:03:31,969
sold about a third more.

63
00:03:32,369 --> 00:03:36,183
A higher price, ending in nine, beat a lower one.

64
00:03:36,783 --> 00:03:40,693
Then there's the crossed-out price. Was ten dollars, now six. That first

65
00:03:40,693 --> 00:03:44,603
number works like an anchor. Once your brain has seen ten, six

66
00:03:44,603 --> 00:03:48,187
feels like a win, whether or not anyone ever paid ten.

67
00:03:48,567 --> 00:03:52,043
The same researchers found that a simple sale sign can boost sales,

68
00:03:52,043 --> 00:03:55,518
sometimes even without much of a price cut, because most of us

69
00:03:55,518 --> 00:03:57,546
don't actually know what things should cost.

70
00:03:58,146 --> 00:04:01,702
Then there are deals like two for five dollars. Lots of people

71
00:04:01,702 --> 00:04:05,259
automatically grab two, even when one would cost the same per item.

72
00:04:05,859 --> 00:04:10,225
And then there's shrinkflation. Instead of raising the price, a company makes

73
00:04:10,225 --> 00:04:14,228
the package a little smaller. Same price. Same bag. Less inside.

74
00:04:14,608 --> 00:04:18,299
Your brain checks the price. It rarely checks the grams.

75
00:04:18,999 --> 00:04:22,605
And then there's you. Your own body can work against your wallet.

76
00:04:23,005 --> 00:04:26,802
A 2015 study found that hungry people didn't just want more food.

77
00:04:26,802 --> 00:04:30,598
They grabbed more non-food things too, and hungry shoppers in a department

78
00:04:30,598 --> 00:04:33,446
store spent more than shoppers who had just eaten.

79
00:04:33,826 --> 00:04:37,684
Hunger seems to flip your brain into a general get-more-stuff mode.

80
00:04:38,284 --> 00:04:42,637
Meanwhile, your loyalty card is quietly taking notes. In 2012, the New

81
00:04:42,637 --> 00:04:46,989
York Times reported that Target had built a pregnancy prediction score, using

82
00:04:46,989 --> 00:04:51,342
purchases like unscented lotion and vitamin supplements to guess which shoppers were

83
00:04:51,342 --> 00:04:52,430
expecting a baby.

84
00:04:52,810 --> 00:04:57,673
So the store isn't just reading the room. It's reading you.

85
00:04:58,673 --> 00:05:03,250
And finally, after all that walking, deciding, and resisting, you reach the

86
00:05:03,250 --> 00:05:03,631
checkout.

87
00:05:04,031 --> 00:05:08,522
You're tired. You're waiting in line. And right at hand height are

88
00:05:08,522 --> 00:05:10,768
candy bars, gum, and cold drinks.

89
00:05:11,268 --> 00:05:14,791
That's no accident. After dozens of small decisions, a quick, easy treat

90
00:05:14,791 --> 00:05:17,140
is a lot harder to say no to.

91
00:05:17,520 --> 00:05:21,598
In fact, this spot works so well that in 2022, England made

92
00:05:21,598 --> 00:05:25,677
it illegal for larger stores to put junk food at checkouts, entrances,

93
00:05:25,677 --> 00:05:27,376
and the ends of aisles.

94
00:05:28,176 --> 00:05:30,800
So here's the real secret.

95
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:35,362
Supermarkets don't force you to buy things you don't want. They make

96
00:05:35,362 --> 00:05:36,377
buying feel automatic.

97
00:05:36,757 --> 00:05:40,565
The long walk, the slow music, the eye-level shelf, the price ending

98
00:05:40,565 --> 00:05:44,373
in nine. Every trick is designed to keep your brain on autopilot,

99
00:05:44,373 --> 00:05:47,863
so you never stop to ask, do I actually need this?

100
00:05:48,463 --> 00:05:51,381
And the moment you do stop and ask, most of the tricks

101
00:05:51,381 --> 00:05:51,867
stop working.

102
00:05:52,967 --> 00:05:56,770
So next time, bring a list. Eat before you go. Look at

103
00:05:56,770 --> 00:06:00,573
the top and bottom shelves. And check the price per unit, not

104
00:06:00,573 --> 00:06:01,841
the big yellow sign.

105
00:06:02,341 --> 00:06:06,208
You won't beat every trick. But now, at least, you'll see them

106
00:06:06,208 --> 00:06:06,530
coming.

107
00:06:06,930 --> 00:06:10,739
And maybe, this time, you'll actually remember the milk.

108
00:06:11,539 --> 00:06:14,871
If this changed how you'll walk through the store, subscribe to Randos

109
00:06:14,871 --> 00:06:18,203
in 60s for more weird stuff about your brain. See you in

110
00:06:18,203 --> 00:06:19,036
the next one.
